#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mixed_radix

#% text
# `mixed_radix([b1, ..., bk])` stands for a base in which the last `k` digits have the bases `b1, ..., bk`; whatever is left forms one leading digit.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `mixed_radix` is inert: it is the base argument of `integer_digits` and `from_digits`.

#% item
# The bases are integers at least 2. The leading digit is not reduced: it holds the quotient left after the last base, as the days in a count of days, hours, minutes and seconds.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
integer_digits(100000, mixed_radix([24, 60, 60]))
#% output
#= [1, 3, 46, 40]

#% cell
from_digits([1, 3, 46, 40], mixed_radix([24, 60, 60]))
#% output
#= 100000

#% cell
integer_digits(1000, mixed_radix([2, 3, 5]))
#% output
#= [33, 0, 2, 0]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Feet and inches from a number of inches (12 inches to the foot):

#% cell
integer_digits(100, mixed_radix([12]))
#% output
#= [8, 4]

#% text
# A count of seconds as days, hours, minutes, seconds, padded to four digits:

#% cell
integer_digits(5000, mixed_radix([24, 60, 60]), 4)
#% output
#= [0, 1, 23, 20]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `integer_digits`, `from_digits`
